I fixed it, and it's committed as 96e4c1f9. When stdout is a pipe, a turn that dies after it has started printing now leaves exactly one copy of its partial text instead of six. Build, clippy, fmt and the full test suite all pass (6195 tests, 0 failed).

Reproduced first, with the test unchanged. On both plain -p and piped stdin, stdout was "PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n\n\n" five times and then "PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n": six requests, six copies, nonzero exit. There was no BEL byte (0x07) in the output, so this morning's bell fix holds.

The rule. If stdout is not a terminal and the attempt that died has already written text to it, yoyo doesn't retry. It exits nonzero with the original error and adds one stderr line saying why, and that the user should rerun the command. Three cases retry exactly as before:
• a terminal (a person can see the restart);
• --print / json output, where stdout is held back for the final answer, so a retry is safe;
• an attempt that died before writing anything (the usual "overloaded" at request start).

Where it applies. One pure function, should_retry_after_partial, in a new small file src/prompt/retry_after_partial.rs, makes the decision. I kept it out of prompt.rs because that file is already over its size limit. Four retry sites call it, not the two the plan named. The two retry arms in the main loop and the image/file loop are the expected ones. The other two are the malformed-tool-call resamples in those same loops: they re-run the same prompt, so they would print the partial text again in the same way.

Tests.
• The existing test now checks the exact stdout bytes (one copy), exactly one request, and the skip line on stderr.
• New near-miss test: the stub server can return a different response per request, so it fails the first request before any text, then succeeds. It confirms the retry still happens, exit is 0, and the answer appears once.
• A 4-row table covers every input combination, and a check on the source confirms all four retry sites call the shared function. That check only proves the calls are there, not that they fire.
• Positive control, run as one command: with the function forced to always allow retries, the exact-output test failed by name. After restoring it, the test passed and no sabotage marker was left in the code.

Not covered:
• **Retry after the context window overflows:** that error is rejected when the request starts and never carries text.
• **Retry after a tool error:** it sends a new follow-up prompt rather than re-running the dead one.

Both are recorded in the new ARCHITECTURE.md entry under prompt.rs. docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md now has a paragraph explaining the no-retry behaviour and why.
